# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# Kernel config fragment for the Samsung Galaxy Tab S7 LTE (SM-T875, gts7l).
# Layer onto the plain arm64 `defconfig` with scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh,
# not meant to stand alone. See docs/kernel-config-notes.md for the audit
# this fragment came out of - what defconfig already covers, what genuinely
# needed changing, and why.
#
# Phase 1 scope only (boot to a console + mount root): this fragment does
# NOT yet enable anything for display, touch, S Pen, charging, or Wi-Fi -
# those aren't needed to reach a shell and are deferred to their own phases
# per plans/roadmap.md, same as kernel/dts/sm8250-samsung-gts7l.dts.

# Root storage (UFS) must be able to come up without depending on module
# load order in an early initramfs - defconfig ships both of these as
# modules (=m). Force them built-in so the very first boot attempt has one
# fewer moving part between "kernel starts" and "root filesystem mounts".
CONFIG_SCSI_UFS_QCOM=y
# PHY_QCOM_QMP_UFS lives under the PHY_QCOM_QMP menuconfig umbrella and is
# capped at that parent's tristate value - the parent must be promoted too,
# or PHY_QCOM_QMP_UFS silently stays =m no matter what this fragment says.
CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_QMP=y
CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_QMP_UFS=y

# CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE + a simple-framebuffer devicetree node reusing the ABL's
# splash region were tried on 2026-09-12 to get an on-screen boot console
# (neither /proc/last_kmsg nor pstore console-ramoops could show our own
# kernel's output - see kernel/dts/sm8250-samsung-gts7l.dts for why). An
# isolated test at the time showed this combination regressed a
# previously-working boot back to Download Mode - but that test predates
# Round 12/13's finding (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md) that flashing *any*
# custom DTB/DTBO at all was the actual cause of every DTB-matching failure
# in this project, independent of framebuffer content. Retrying 2026-09-17
# (Round 14) with the framebuffer node patched directly into the *exact*
# stock DTB bytes that just passed ABL cleanly in Round 13 (fdtput, not a
# DTS rebuild) - qcom,msm-id/board-id/pmic-id and everything else
# untouched, so this is a real single-variable test this time.
CONFIG_FB_SIMPLE=y

# Round 22 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): force the cmdline
# directly into the kernel, independent of whatever devicetree bootargs
# merging or uniLoader's own patch_dtb() (which only ever adds
# linux,initrd-start/-end, never bootargs) end up doing. Belt-and-suspenders
# alongside the DTS's own chosen/bootargs override.
#
# Round 32: dropped softlockup_panic=1/watchdog_thresh=5 - Round 30/31
# confirmed the kernel now genuinely boots to `Run /init`, and every test
# since has hit a clean, silent warm reset right after this script's own
# last line, with no panic text and no new crash-dump entry - not
# matching a captured lockup-detector panic at all. Removing this
# alongside simplifying the init script itself
# (kernel/initramfs/init) to isolate the real cause rather than leaving
# a config that was never actually explaining the symptom in place.
#
# Round 33: the owner caught real boot text on the physical LCD panel via
# slow-motion video (simple-framebuffer genuinely works, briefly) - but
# the screen goes black immediately after "fb0: simplefb registered!"
# prints, even though the kernel keeps running fine afterward (confirmed
# via /proc/last_kmsg reaching "Run /init" normally). Online research
# (per the owner's suggestion) pointed at a well-known class of problem
# for exactly this symptom on Qualcomm/mainline handoffs: Linux
# automatically disables ("gates") any clock with no driver claiming it
# as in-use, late in boot (clk_disable_unused()) - if ABL left the
# display controller's own clocks running to actively drive the panel,
# and this minimal kernel has no MDSS/display driver to claim them
# (Phase 2 scope, not yet added), the kernel would gate them right around
# the same late-boot point we're seeing the screen go black at.
# `clk_ignore_unused` is the standard, well-known mainline cmdline
# parameter for this exact class of problem - tells the clock framework
# to leave whatever the bootloader left running alone.
#
# Crash forensics trial (docs/crash-investigation-nmi-research.md,
# 2026-09-20/21): irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1 is the actual runtime switch
# for CONFIG_ARM64_PSEUDO_NMI below - the Kconfig symbol alone changes
# nothing at boot without it (drivers/irqchip/irq-gic-v3.c only calls
# static_branch_enable(&supports_pseudo_nmis) when this param is set).
# Added here, not just in the DTS's own &{/chosen}/bootargs, because this
# is the string that actually reaches the kernel (CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=y
# below wins over whatever devicetree bootargs say - the DTS copy is
# belt-and-suspenders only, per this file's own Round 22 note). Meant to
# be dropped again once the pseudo-NMI trial is done (~5% runtime cost
# while active, per the research doc) - not a permanent addition.
# rootflags=nodelalloc (2026-09-21, docs/load-test-modules.md/
# kernel-boot-debugging.md "Root cause found"): testing whether
# disabling ext4 delayed allocation avoids the real spinlock stuck in
# ext4_da_reserve_space() under heavy write load (storage_load.sh
# reproduces in seconds). Confirmed the hard way that this can NOT be
# applied via `mount -o remount` or /etc/fstab - ext4 explicitly refuses
# ("can't disable delalloc during remount") since root is already
# mounted with delalloc by the time fstab is processed, both manually
# and via systemd-remount-fs at boot. rootflags on the kernel cmdline
# is applied at the kernel's own initial mount of root, before that
# restriction can apply.
# 2026-09-22: `mem=2G` diagnostic for the CPU hard-hang investigation
# (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md, "CPU hard-hang, reserved-memory/
# carveout boundary test") - tried and reverted. Result: the exact
# same RCU-stall/"CPUs haven't responded" freeze fired within ~10s of
# ordinary boot (systemd/Bluetooth/WiFi coming up, no deliberate
# memory stress at all, before the USB-gadget network service even
# started) - the opposite of what the reserved-memory/carveout-overlap
# theory would predict (a smaller cap should make hitting a mismatched
# high-address carveout boundary LESS likely, not more). Real result:
# 2G is simply tight enough that routine boot-time memory pressure
# alone trips the same underlying bug - evidence against a specific
# physical-address mismatch, and for general memory-pressure
# sensitivity regardless of where the pressure originates. Reverted
# because it made the device unreliable even for ordinary boots.
CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=tty0 loglevel=15 clk_ignore_unused irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1 rootflags=nodelalloc"
CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=y

# Round 24 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the memory-node fix
# eliminated the OOM panic/watchdog-reset loop from Round 20-23, but the
# kernel is now hanging silently instead - no new entry in the
# /dev/block/by-name/debug crash-dump history at all, meaning nothing is
# detecting the hang and calling panic(). Confirmed CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR
# and CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR were both off in the working .config.
# Enabling both plus CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC and
# CONFIG_PANIC_ON_OOPS turns a future silent hang back into a captured,
# analyzable crash automatically (matching how the Round 23 OOM panic was
# actually found - Samsung's crash-dump capture already works, it just
# needs the kernel to actually call panic() to trigger it) - no UART
# needed. watchdog_thresh=5 shortens the default ~20s soft-lockup
# detection window for faster iteration.
CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC=y
CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HARDLOCKUP_PANIC=y
CONFIG_PANIC_ON_OOPS=y
CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT=5

# Round 32 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): USB gadget serial
# console, now that Round 30/31 got the kernel booting to a real shell -
# gives live, interactive access instead of the reboot-and-read-last_kmsg
# cycle this whole debugging effort has depended on, and avoids needing a
# real display/panel driver (a much bigger Phase 2 undertaking) just to
# interact with the system. Most of the real infrastructure was already
# on by default: CONFIG_USB_DWC3/CONFIG_USB_GADGET/
# CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_ACM were already =y, and the devicetree already has
# a real `usb_1_dwc3`/`qcom,sm8250-dwc3` node with proper PHY references.
# The one real gap: CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS defaulted to =m (a module) - no
# good for a minimal initramfs with no module-loading support at all.
# Force it built-in; the init script (kernel/initramfs/init) does the
# actual configfs gadget setup (create gadget, ACM function, bind UDC).
CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS=y

# Round 36 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): the real reason
# /sys/class/udc stayed empty (no USB gadget console) even after Round 32's
# CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS fix - found directly from this device's own last_kmsg
# after adding automatic USB diagnostics to the init script (no working
# input device to type at that shell interactively - a USB-OTG keyboard
# doesn't work either, correctly, since dr_mode = "peripheral" disables
# host mode entirely). The log showed exactly why:
# `platform a600000.usb: deferred probe pending: dwc3: failed to
# initialize core` / `platform: supplier 88e3000.phy not ready`. dwc3
# itself is fine - it's permanently waiting on its USB2 HS PHY
# (`usb_1_hsphy`, compatible "qcom,usb-snps-hs-7nm-phy"), which is never
# going to appear: its driver, CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_USB_SNPS_FEMTO_V2, defaults
# to =m, and this minimal initramfs has no module-loading support at all -
# the exact same class of bug as CONFIG_DRM_MSM=m never loading. Force it
# built-in.
CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_USB_SNPS_FEMTO_V2=y

# Phase 2 (2026-09-19, docs/phase2-panel-scoping.md): display bring-up.
# CONFIG_DRM/CONFIG_DRM_MSM both default to =m - the exact same
# no-module-loading problem as CONFIG_DRM_PANEL_NOVATEK_NT36523/
# CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_USB_SNPS_FEMTO_V2 before it (Rounds 35/37). Everything
# under DRM_MSM's own umbrella (DPU, DSI, the 7nm PHY) was already =y in
# defconfig, capped at =m by the parent - same parent/child tristate
# ceiling gotcha already documented in docs/kernel-config-notes.md from
# the Phase 1 UFS PHY work. CONFIG_I2C_QCOM_GENI needs the same fix,
# since the ISL98608 bias IC lives on a real hardware QUP I2C bus
# (&i2c8), and CONFIG_BACKLIGHT_CLASS_DEVICE because
# nt36523_create_backlight() (this panel uses DCS backlight, no
# external chip) needs that subsystem built in to register at all.
CONFIG_DRM=y
CONFIG_DRM_KMS_HELPER=y
CONFIG_DRM_DISPLAY_HELPER=y
# DRM_MSM's own Kconfig has `depends on QCOM_LLCC || QCOM_LLCC=n` (and
# the same for QCOM_OCMEM) - a built-in driver can't depend on a
# loadable module, so leaving these at defconfig's =m silently capped
# DRM_MSM back down to =m even with the line below - found by checking
# the actual post-olddefconfig .config rather than assuming the fragment
# alone was enough.
CONFIG_QCOM_LLCC=y
CONFIG_QCOM_OCMEM=y
CONFIG_DRM_MSM=y
CONFIG_I2C_QCOM_GENI=y
CONFIG_BACKLIGHT_CLASS_DEVICE=y

# Round 47 (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): `&i2c8`
# (`CONFIG_I2C_QCOM_GENI=y` above) never actually probed at all -
# confirmed directly by the owner via a live shell,
# `ls /sys/bus/i2c/devices/` came back completely empty. Flagged this
# exact risk back in Round 41's notes (`&i2c8` declares GPI DMA
# channels via `dmas = <&gpi_dma1 ...>`) but decided against fixing it
# preemptively since "no i2c8 errors appeared in dmesg" - that silence
# was itself the real signal, not evidence of success: `i2c-qcom-geni.c`
# only needs a working DMA channel if this specific GENI SE instance's
# hardware FIFO is disabled, and if it is, `dma_request_chan()`
# returns -EPROBE_DEFER forever when no DMA channel provider driver
# is registered - a deferred probe prints nothing by design, matching
# total silence exactly, not a successful FIFO-mode probe as assumed.
# Same no-module-loading bug pattern as DRM_MSM/
# PHY_QCOM_USB_SNPS_FEMTO_V2/REGULATOR_QCOM_REFGEN before it (Rounds
# 35/37/40) - the fourth instance of it in this project.
CONFIG_QCOM_GPI_DMA=y

# Round 39 test (2026-09-19, docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md): found via the
# USB gadget console's own dmesg (finally usable as a live debug tool) -
# `msm_dsi: Failed to get supply 'refgen'` on both DSI hosts, and
# `DSI PLL(0) lock failed, status=0x00000000` right alongside it. The
# `refgen` devicetree node itself (sm8250.dtsi's `qcom,sm8250-refgen-
# regulator`) has no status override needed, already enabled by default -
# but its actual driver, CONFIG_REGULATOR_QCOM_REFGEN, defaults to =m,
# the exact same no-module-loading bug as CONFIG_DRM_MSM and
# CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_USB_SNPS_FEMTO_V2 before it (Rounds 35/37/38) - a real
# driver exists (drivers/regulator/qcom-refgen-regulator.c), it just
# never gets to register. Likely also the direct cause of the PLL lock
# failure, since refgen ("reference generator") plausibly supplies the
# bias/reference voltage the DSI PHY's analog PLL needs to lock at all -
# not yet confirmed, but a strong, testable hypothesis given both
# symptoms appeared together and trace to the same missing driver.
CONFIG_REGULATOR_QCOM_REFGEN=y

# The actual new panel driver entry (drivers/gpu/drm/panel/
# panel-novatek-nt36523.c, compatible "samsung,gts7l-ppa957db1-nt36523")
# and this device's own ISL98608 bias IC driver
# (drivers/misc/isl98608-gts7l.c) - neither exists in defconfig at all
# since both are new additions for this device.
CONFIG_DRM_PANEL_NOVATEK_NT36523=y
CONFIG_ISL98608_GTS7L=y

# This device's own NT36523 TDDI touchscreen driver
# (drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36523-gts7l.c, compatible
# "novatek,nt36523-ts") - same physical chip as the panel above, new
# addition for this device, not in defconfig.
CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_NT36523_GTS7L=y

# 0004-touchscreen-firmware-flash-fix.patch: this chip needs its
# firmware flashed at every boot (real, vendor-confirmed CRC-fail
# auto-reboot behavior without it - see the patch and
# docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md). Built directly into the kernel image
# rather than relying on a userspace /lib/firmware path, so
# request_firmware() never depends on rootfs mount timing - copy
# kernel/firmware/tsp_novatek/nt36523_gts7l.bin (tracked in this repo;
# the gts7l-specific one - a same-named but different file also exists
# under references/kernel_samsung_sm8250/, not used) into
# work/linux/firmware/tsp_novatek/ before building.
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE="tsp_novatek/nt36523_gts7l.bin"
CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE_DIR="firmware"

# Phase 3 Wi-Fi/BT, continued: real PCIe enumeration confirmed on
# hardware (round75-build - QCA6390 shows up as a genuine PCI device,
# `ath11k_pci` claims it, MHI powers on), failing only on the very last
# step: ath11k/QCA6390/hw2.0/amss.bin missing. Pulled this exact unit's
# own real firmware directly off its `vendor` partition (mounted via
# /dev/block/mapper/vendor in TWRP, same precedent as the Adreno
# zap-shader) - `/vendor/firmware/qca6390/amss20.bin` (ELF, matches
# ath11k's own ATH11K_AMSS_FILE expectation directly, just renamed),
# `m3.bin`/`regdb.bin` (filenames already match ath11k's own
# ATH11K_M3_FILE/ATH11K_REGDB_FILE_NAME exactly), and
# `bdwlan.elf` -> `board.bin` (a raw ELF board-data blob, matching
# ath11k_core_fetch_board_data_api_1()'s plain-bytes single-file
# fallback format, not the newer multi-variant `board-2.bin`
# container). All four tracked at kernel/firmware/ath11k/QCA6390/hw2.0/
# - but, unlike the touch firmware above, deliberately NOT added to
# CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE: round76-build tried that and its packaged
# boot.img came out 825408 bytes (806 KiB) larger than the `boot`
# partition's real, fixed capacity (confirmed by a real `dd` ENOSPC
# error on real hardware, boot image safely restored to round75
# immediately after) - round75's kernel was already filling almost the
# entire partition with zero headroom left, so baking in ~4.5MB of
# Wi-Fi firmware the same way touch's firmware was baked in simply
# doesn't fit. Unlike touch (which needs its firmware at the very
# first probe, before anything else can even talk to that I2C bus),
# ath11k probes well after the real Arch rootfs is mounted, so a real
# /lib/firmware/ath11k/QCA6390/hw2.0/ path on the persistent rootfs
# works fine timing-wise and doesn't touch the kernel image size at
# all - install these four tracked files there instead.

# Phase 3 Wi-Fi/BT (docs/phase3-wifi-bt-scoping.md): ATH11K/ATH11K_PCI/
# MHI_BUS/PCIE_QCOM are all already on via plain defconfig - the one
# real gap is the QCA6390 PMU power-sequencing driver
# (drivers/power/sequencing/pwrseq-qcom-wcn.c, compatible
# "qcom,qca6390-pmu"). Despite `default m if ARCH_QCOM` in its own
# Kconfig, it wasn't coming on via defconfig alone - stated explicitly
# here like every other gts7l-specific symbol in this fragment.
CONFIG_POWER_SEQUENCING_QCOM_WCN=y

# Forced built-in (=y, not defconfig's =m) for this first bring-up test.
# This project has never loaded a kernel module at runtime - the
# initramfs (kernel/initramfs/init) has zero modprobe/insmod/depmod
# infrastructure - so a module here would just silently never load,
# indistinguishable from "the DTS/hardware path doesn't work at all".
# Isolates that variable for this first test; real module loading (now
# that Phase 5 has a persistent Arch rootfs with systemd) is a real,
# deliberately deferred follow-up per docs/phase3-wifi-bt-scoping.md,
# not decided yet.
# CFG80211/MAC80211 are ATH11K's own parent Kconfig deps - a real
# tristate-ceiling gotcha this project already hit once before
# (docs/kernel-config-notes.md): requesting a child symbol =y while its
# parent stays =m from plain defconfig just silently caps the child back
# down to =m, not an error. Both forced =y too, not just ATH11K itself.
# CFG80211 itself has a further, one-more-level-removed ceiling:
# `depends on RFKILL || !RFKILL` evaluates to the *tristate* value of
# RFKILL when RFKILL=m (m || n = m, not a plain boolean short-circuit),
# so RFKILL=m (plain defconfig's default) was silently capping CFG80211
# at =m even with CFG80211=y requested directly here - confirmed by
# checking .config directly after a first attempt without this line
# still showed CFG80211=m. RFKILL forced =y too, for the same reason.
CONFIG_RFKILL=y
CONFIG_CFG80211=y
CONFIG_MAC80211=y
CONFIG_ATH11K=y
CONFIG_ATH11K_PCI=y
CONFIG_MHI_BUS=y
CONFIG_MHI_BUS_PCI_GENERIC=y

# Real hardware test (round74-build) found one more link in the same
# tristate-ceiling chain: &pcie0's own PHY (phy@1c06000, compatible
# "qcom,sm8250-qmp-gen3x1-pcie-phy") had NO driver bound at all -
# CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_QMP_PCIE was still =m from plain defconfig (missed
# earlier since it's not an ATH11K/CFG80211 dependency at the Kconfig
# level - it's a *devicetree* supplier link, `1c00000.pcie`'s "phys ="
# property pointing at this phy, invisible to a plain Kconfig
# dependency read). Confirmed live via
# /sys/devices/platform/soc@0/1c06000.phy having no `driver` symlink
# and `1c00000.pcie` sitting permanently in
# /sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred with an empty reason (silently
# waiting on its phy_get() call, which never printed anything itself).
CONFIG_PHY_QCOM_QMP_PCIE=y

# Bluetooth (docs/phase3-bluetooth-scoping.md) - same tristate-ceiling
# gotcha class as ATH11K/CFG80211/RFKILL/PHY_QCOM_QMP_PCIE above, hit a
# third time. CONFIG_SERIAL_QCOM_GENI (the &uart6 controller itself)
# was already =y from defconfig; these three were still =m despite
# CONFIG_BT_HCIUART_QCA (the actual QCA-protocol glue) already being
# on.
CONFIG_BT=y
CONFIG_BT_QCA=y
CONFIG_BT_HCIUART=y

# Real hardware test (round80-build, local-bd-address fix already
# landed and confirmed working - a real BLE mouse pairs and shows
# "Connected: yes" in bluetoothctl) found the actual remaining gap:
# CONFIG_UHID was completely unset. BlueZ's own "input" plugin bridges
# BLE HID-over-GATT (HOGP) devices - like this mouse, which only
# advertises the "Human Interface Device" GATT service, not the
# classic BR/EDR HID profile - into the kernel's real input subsystem
# via /dev/uhid, injecting synthetic HID reports read over GATT.
# Without it, bluetoothd completes the BLE connection and GATT
# discovery fine (hence "Connected: yes"), but has no path at all to
# deliver actual mouse movement/click events - exactly matching the
# real symptom reported ("says connected but doesn't work"). Also
# forcing CONFIG_BT_HIDP=y (was =m, same tristate-ceiling pattern -
# needed for classic BR/EDR HID devices, e.g. this device's own Book
# Cover Keyboard trackpad might use it) and CONFIG_HIDRAW=y (was unset;
# commonly needed by userspace HID tooling, harmless to include) while
# already in this area.
CONFIG_UHID=y
CONFIG_HIDRAW=y

# docs/phase5-module-loading-scoping.md: real module-loading trial run
# - CONFIG_BT_HIDP picked deliberately as the very first thing built as
# a real loadable module (=m, not =y like everything else this
# project has ever added; CONFIG_HIDRAW was tried first but turned out
# to be a plain `bool` in this kernel version, not tristate - can't be
# a module at all, reverted above). BT_HIDP is genuinely harmless to
# test with: it's classic BR/EDR HID support, and nothing working
# today actually exercises it - the real BLE mouse already confirmed
# working goes through CONFIG_UHID/BlueZ's HOGP bridge instead,
# deliberately left alone above. Proves the whole build ->
# modules_install -> deploy -> depmod -> udev-autoload pipeline
# end-to-end against a known-good, low-stakes baseline before audio
# (the real motivation for this whole effort) touches it.
CONFIG_BT_HIDP=m

# Phase 5 follow-up: real hardware use found `pacman` fails every
# single install with "switching to sandbox user 'alpm' failed!" -
# pacman 7.x sandboxes its download/extract step with Landlock, and
# CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK was never enabled (plain defconfig doesn't
# turn it on, and nothing in this project's own fragment had touched
# it before now). A pure bool, only depends on the already-`=y`
# CONFIG_SECURITY - no tristate-ceiling gotcha here, just never added.
CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK=y

# Charging status (docs/phase3-battery-scoping.md) - charger/status
# only, not battery-percentage/fuel-gauge (mainline has no driver for
# that at all for this chip - a much bigger, deliberately deferred
# task). Both depend on the already-satisfied I2C core; no
# tristate-ceiling chain expected here, but forced =y anyway to match
# this project's established policy (no module-loading
# infrastructure) rather than trusting defconfig's default.
CONFIG_MFD_MAX77705=y
CONFIG_CHARGER_MAX77705=y

# Battery percentage / fuel gauge (docs/phase3-fuelgauge-scoping.md) -
# not a driver port, real prior art already merged in mainline's
# shared MAX17042-family driver (a "maxim,max77705-battery" compatible
# entry already exists in drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c).
# tristate, depends on I2C (already =y), selects REGMAP_I2C - no
# tristate-ceiling chain expected.
CONFIG_BATTERY_MAX17042=y

# Real, severe stability bug found under sustained real-world load
# (Firefox, Minecraft) - a hard CPU lockup (watchdog-detected,
# "CPU3: Watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 4"), preceded by a DPU
# display frame-event queue overflow storm
# ([drm:dpu_crtc_frame_event_cb] *ERROR* crtc91 event 1 overflow).
# Root-caused, not guessed: `qcom-cpufreq-hw: Failed to find icc
# paths` has been a permanently-deferred probe in *every single boot
# this entire session* (confirmed via journalctl on every prior
# round's boot log, never investigated until now) - meaning real CPU
# frequency scaling has never worked at all, the whole session; CPUs
# have been stuck at whatever fixed frequency ABL/XBL left them at
# since first boot. Traced directly: cpufreq-hw's own icc-path lookup
# (dev_pm_opp_of_find_icc_paths(), drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c)
# needs the `epss_l3` interconnect provider node
# (arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250.dtsi's `interconnect@18590000`,
# referenced by every CPU node's own `interconnects` property) to
# actually probe - and its driver, CONFIG_INTERCONNECT_QCOM_OSM_L3,
# was the *only* interconnect driver in this entire kernel's whole
# `CONFIG_INTERCONNECT_QCOM_*` family still left at defconfig's `=m`
# (every sibling SoC's own driver defaults to `=y` in plain
# defconfig - a real, apparently-unintentional defconfig outlier, not
# a deliberate choice) - and this project had no module-loading
# infrastructure until this exact session, so it could never load.
# Real-world heavy load (Firefox, Minecraft) on CPUs stuck at a fixed,
# likely-conservative boot-time frequency is a well-understood, real
# way to starve a real-time display kthread worker badly enough to
# both overflow its bounded event queue and eventually trip a genuine
# hard-lockup watchdog - matches the observed symptom chain directly.
# Forced built-in (not left as a module even now that module-loading
# infra exists) since this is foundational, performance-critical
# infrastructure needed from early boot, not a late-attaching optional
# peripheral - matches this project's own stated policy (docs/
# phase5-module-loading-scoping.md) of keeping bootstrap-critical
# pieces built-in and reserving modules for genuinely optional/late
# hardware.
CONFIG_INTERCONNECT_QCOM_OSM_L3=y

# Crash forensics (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md) - the OSM_L3 fix
# above did NOT stop the real crash (confirmed on real hardware: same
# "crtc91 event 1 overflow" -> watchdog hard-lockup signature recurred
# under Minecraft's real 3D rendering load even with CPU frequency
# scaling genuinely working). The hard reset kills the device before
# the kernel can flush a real backtrace to the journal, leaving the
# actual stuck function unknown - pstore/ramoops (a persistent RAM
# region that survives a hard reset) exists specifically to preserve
# whatever the kernel wrote right up to the lockup. The devicetree side
# was quietly already correct the whole time (sm8250-samsung-common.dtsi's
# own real `ramoops@9fa00000` node) - only the kernel config was ever
# missing. PSTORE_RAM is the backend matching that DT node;
# PSTORE_CONSOLE is the important one - continuous console capture,
# not just panic-time dumps, so even a hard lockup that never reaches
# a clean panic() path should still have everything written up to
# that point preserved.
CONFIG_PSTORE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE=y

# Real hardware test found pstore/ramoops alone isn't enough for a
# real hard-lockup crash specifically: the abrupt reset can leave the
# persistent-RAM header torn (confirmed live: "ramoops: found existing
# invalid buffer, size 155976, start 155977" - `start` one byte past
# `size`, a classic non-atomic-header-update-vs-abrupt-reset race, not
# a checksum/ECC-fixable bit-flip), causing the pstore driver to
# reject a real ~152KB of otherwise-probably-intact captured console
# data on the very next boot. Disabling STRICT_DEVMEM (was =y, plain
# defconfig default) allows a raw /dev/mem read of the ramoops
# physical region as a manual fallback when that happens - bypassing
# the driver's own conservative all-or-nothing validation to recover
# whatever's actually still there. A real, deliberate, single-owner
# debug-device tradeoff (this project's whole kernel is already
# custom/unsigned/debug-oriented by design), not a default worth
# recommending generally.
# CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM is not set

# Crash forensics, continued (docs/crash-investigation-nmi-research.md) -
# the recovered pstore backtrace from a real hard-lockup showed CPU 5
# never responded even to an NMI sent from CPU 4, then never responded to
# the SMP stop-CPU shutdown request either - genuinely frozen, three
# independent ways, but we still don't know what CPU 5 was actually
# executing, because the current detector (CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_BUDDY,
# the only one available without this) is a *different*, unaffected CPU
# noticing CPU 5 stopped reporting in - it can never capture CPU 5's own
# registers/stack. ARM64_PSEUDO_NMI is the one real, already-upstream,
# no-patch-carrying path to a true per-CPU NMI-capable detector
# (HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_PERF) on GICv3 hardware (which SM8250 has) - but
# whether it actually works depends on TrustZone/`xbl`/`tz` firmware
# having set SCR_EL3.FIQ=1, which this project has no visibility into and
# will never touch - answerable only empirically, by enabling it and
# checking dmesg for "Pseudo-NMIs enabled" vs. a silent no-op.
# Deliberately build+bootarg-gated, not left on by default: the actual
# runtime behavior only changes once `irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1` is also
# passed on the kernel cmdline (see &{/chosen}'s bootargs in
# kernel/dts/sm8250-samsung-gts7l.dts) - enabling just this Kconfig
# symbol alone costs nothing at runtime. Real, measured cost *if* the
# bootarg is also set and firmware actually supports it: ~5% on general
# workloads, so only meant to be flipped on for a deliberate crash
# reproduction session, not left on permanently.
CONFIG_ARM64_PSEUDO_NMI=y
CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_PERF=y

# Freeze investigation, continued (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md) -
# Qualcomm EUD (Embedded USB Debugger), a separate hardware debug block
# reachable over the same USB-C port, independent of the MUIC/CCIC
# switch a raw I2C attempt already failed to move. Real downstream
# source for this exact device (references/gts7l) ships its own EUD
# node `status = "ok"` - Samsung shipped this hardware enabled, not
# fused off. Forced built-in rather than left as a module (`=m` at
# plain defconfig) since it's needed from boot for this debug purpose,
# not a late-attaching peripheral we'd otherwise leave modular.
# CONFIG_QCOM_SCM and CONFIG_USB_ROLE_SWITCH (both dependencies) were
# already =y at plain defconfig - nothing else to force.
CONFIG_USB_QCOM_EUD=y

# Freeze investigation, continued (docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md, "Crash
# #4"/"Crash #5") - the pseudo-NMI detector above never fires for this
# freeze at all, and pstore captures nothing, twice, on independent real
# repros. Research (a downstream-vs-mainline diff, docs/load-test-modules.md)
# found the exact same bug class as CONFIG_INTERCONNECT_QCOM_OSM_L3 above,
# on the DDR-bandwidth side instead of CPU L3: CONFIG_QCOM_ICC_BWMON was
# left at defconfig's `=m` and confirmed never loaded on real hardware
# (`lsmod` empty, no bwmon devfreq device under /sys/class/devfreq/ at
# all - only `1d84000.ufshc` and `3d00000.gpu` exist). Mainline's own
# sm8250.dtsi already ships a complete `qcom,sm8250-llcc-bwmon` node
# (`pmu@9091000`) that watches aggregate DDR traffic from every bus
# master (CPU + GPU + everything else combined) and scales DDR
# frequency/bandwidth in response - currently completely inert. This
# mechanically fits the observed signature better than a GPU-only
# explanation: it's specifically the *combined* CPU+GPU traffic case that
# needs adaptive DDR bandwidth, a stalled bus/NoC transaction leaves CPUs
# architecturally alive (not looping, so no lockup detector fires) but
# blocked on ordinary loads/stores to any device sharing that fabric
# (display, USB/SSH, DDR itself - explaining why display and SSH die
# simultaneously), and RPMh/BCM-level bus timeouts are independently
# documented upstream as producing at most a bare WARN_ON with no pstore
# trace, not a panic. Forced built-in for the same reason as OSM_L3
# above: foundational, needed from early boot to track real traffic
# patterns, not a late-attaching optional peripheral.
CONFIG_QCOM_ICC_BWMON=y

# Freeze investigation, continued - found a real candidate root cause by
# checking the local reference projects (per the owner's direction):
# references/ubuntu-galaxy-tab-s9-ultra/docs/gpu-recovery.md documents a
# genuine deadlock in mainline's own Freedreno GPU-fault-recovery path
# (a6xx_gmu.c/a6xx_hfi.c's unbounded `wait_for_completion(&fault_coredump_
# done)`, held while `recover_worker()`/`msm_gpu_fault_crashstate_capture()`
# both need the same `gpu->lock`) - confirmed present in this project's
# own tree line-for-line, not just theirs. This is a genuine software
# deadlock (blocked on a mutex/completion), not a hardware hang - a
# structurally different failure class from anything the pseudo-NMI
# hardlockup detector above watches for (that only catches a CPU stuck
# NOT taking interrupts, not a schedulable-but-blocked task). Enabling
# purely as a diagnostic first, before committing to the S9 Ultra
# project's own bounded-wait patch as an actual fix - if this deadlock is
# really happening, this should finally produce a real blocked-task
# stack trace via a scheduler-side timer check that doesn't depend on any
# of the bus/NoC/pstore machinery that's failed to capture anything
# across every real repro attempt so far.
CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK=y

# Freeze investigation, continued (2026-09-21): ruled out cpuidle/PSCI at
# every level (cluster AND per-CPU idle-state collapse both disabled via
# DTS, froze anyway) and a specific unmerged genpd/OSI fix (didn't help).
# A pstore capture caught a real, corroborated (matches a known upstream
# code path) soft-lockup signature: sshd-auth's seccomp BPF filter JIT
# doing self-modifying code via __text_poke -> kick_all_cpus_sync, an
# ordinary IPI broadcast - but a plain IPI cannot reach a CPU with
# PSTATE.DAIF interrupts masked, only NMI-class delivery can. So the
# real question is why some CPU gets stuck in an IRQ-disabled region
# under memory pressure in the first place; any cross-call (BPF JIT
# sync, TLB flush, anything) would hang identically once that happens.
# CONFIG_IRQSOFF_TRACER exists exactly to answer this without needing
# to catch a live freeze in a narrow sampling window - it continuously
# records the single longest IRQs-disabled stretch and its exact code
# path. CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER is its prerequisite.
CONFIG_FTRACE=y
CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER=y
CONFIG_IRQSOFF_TRACER=y

# Panic-on-hang sysctls (softlockup_panic/hung_task_panic/panic_on_rcu_
# stall, set at runtime) got the freeze to actually panic and write a
# pstore dmesg-ramoops record instead of hanging silently - real
# progress. But CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS's deflate encoding turned out to
# be strictly worse for this project's specific failure mode: the
# console-ramoops backend (plain text) survives the read-time bit
# corruption we keep seeing from this device's persistent-RAM region
# well enough to still read around (individual garbled characters, rest
# legible) - but a single bit flip anywhere in a deflate stream corrupts
# everything after it, and the one dmesg-ramoops dump captured so far
# was completely unparseable by zlib in every wbits mode tried. Trading
# a small amount of on-disk size for guaranteed-plain-text panic dumps.
# CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS is independently selectable (fs/pstore/Kconfig)
# by design.
# CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS is not set

# Freeze investigation, continued (2026-09-21): after ruling out
# cpuidle/PSCI (every level), cpufreq/RPMh bandwidth-vote churn
# (performance governor still froze), and treating a GPU-scheduler
# mutex block and an RPMh completion timeout as downstream symptoms
# rather than root causes (different subsystems show up as "blocked"
# in different capture runs - the signature of one shared stall being
# observed through whichever detector happens to catch it first, not
# several independent bugs) - one thing is common to every single
# repro that's frozen this device: they all request more memory than
# is physically available, with zero swap configured (`free -h` showed
# `Swap: 0B` throughout). CONFIG_ZRAM was never enabled at all. Stock
# Android ships zram-backed swap by default on virtually every modern
# device - this is a real, load-bearing difference from stock, not
# just a missing nice-to-have. Built-in (=y, not =m) for the same
# reason as other early-boot-critical drivers in this fragment: this
# project's minimal initramfs doesn't reliably load modules, and swap
# needs to be available before it matters, not after. See
# docs/load-test-modules.md for the reasoning and the test this backs.
CONFIG_ZRAM=y

# Power/volume-down buttons never worked at all under Linux - traced
# directly, not guessed: pm8150.dtsi's real `pon@800` node (compatible
# "qcom,pm8998-pon") is enabled correctly and its children (`pwrkey`/
# `resin`, compatible "qcom,pm8941-pwrkey"/"qcom,pm8941-resin",
# CONFIG_INPUT_PM8941_PWRKEY already =y) show up fine in the compiled
# DTB and under /sys/firmware/devicetree - but no platform_device for
# either ever gets created at all (confirmed live:
# /sys/bus/platform/drivers/pm8941-pwrkey/ has zero bound devices, and
# no pwrkey/resin node exists anywhere under /sys/devices). The actual
# `qcom,pm8998-pon` driver itself (drivers/power/reset/qcom-pon.c) is
# what walks those DT children into real devices via its own probe()
# calling devm_of_platform_populate() - and CONFIG_POWER_RESET_QCOM_PON
# was still at defconfig's `=m`, the exact same no-module-loading bug
# class as every other =m entry in this fragment (DRM_MSM, USB PHY,
# refgen, GPI DMA, OSM_L3, ICC_BWMON before it) - simply never added
# for this one. Force built-in.
CONFIG_POWER_RESET_QCOM_PON=y

# Freeze investigation, continued (2026-09-22): a real, external
# comparison test finally isolated this from a hardware/firmware
# erratum. Wrote a minimal libc-free ARM64 reproducer replicating the
# project's own stress-ng --vm memory-pressure repro
# (docs/load-test-modules.md) and ran it on *stock Android*, on this
# exact physical device - stock survived every level tested, up to and
# including genuine severe memory pressure (`MemFree` down to ~156MB,
# real reclaim/LMK activity), with zero responsiveness lag throughout.
# The one confirmed, real structural difference: stock has no
# `/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/` at all and no `khugepaged`
# process - THP is disabled in Samsung's own production kernel config,
# while plain defconfig defaults to `CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_ALWAYS`
# (khugepaged scanning constantly in the background). This matches this
# project's own earlier-found root cause exactly:
# `khugepaged -> lru_add_drain_all()` blocking on an unresponsive CPU's
# workqueue. Online research (per the owner's direction) found this is
# a real, currently-open, unmerged upstream Linux regression - not
# Qualcomm/Samsung-specific at all, reproducible on plain AWS Graviton3
# ARM64 servers too (a 2026-04 LKML bug report, "lru_add_drain_per_cpu
# barrier work item... pending... yet the work is never dispatched" -
# idle per-CPU workqueue workers failing to pick up pending drain work).
# A later (2026-08) mm/fbatch rework patch series aimed at removing the
# need for lru_add_drain_all() in this path altogether cites the exact
# same symptom ("blocked on an unresponsive CPU to run its workqueue")
# but is still in review, not merged - no upstream fix available yet.
# Given stock's own real-world-proven-stable config already avoids this
# specific trigger entirely, disabling THP here matches that proven
# configuration rather than waiting on an unmerged upstream fix.
CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=n

# Phase 4, S Pen (docs/phase4-spen-scoping.md): mainline already has
# the right driver family (wacom_w9000.c) for this device's Wacom EMR
# digitizer - confirmed field-for-field against the downstream driver,
# not assumed. Needed a new variant entry + tilt-reporting support,
# added as a real patch, not just this config line. Built-in for the
# same reason as other input drivers in this fragment - no reliable
# module loading in this project's minimal initramfs.
CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_WACOM_W9000=y
